<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pacific War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pacific War was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> fought in Asia and the Pacific from 1941 to 1945, chiefly between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. After the US <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/us-oil-embargo-against-japan">oil embargo</a> of 1941, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> launched a wide offensive and by mid-1942 controlled a vast area. Japan's defeat in 1945 ended the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pacific-war.jpg" alt="Pacific War" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joe Rosenthal · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raising_the_Flag_on_Iwo_Jima,_larger_-_edit1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Pacific War was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> fought between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, the Pacific and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Oceans</a> and Oceania. It was the largest theatre geographically and included some of the largest naval battles in history. War between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> had begun in 1937, and Japan had invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1931, but the Pacific War is more widely held to have begun in 1941, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom were drawn in. Japanese strategists had concluded as early as 1935 that the oil of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> was crucial to expansion, and Japan invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a> in 1940.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 7 and 8 December 1941 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> struck simultaneously at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, Guam, Wake Island, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>. At Pearl Harbor eight American battleships were put out of action and 2,403 Americans were killed, but the three American aircraft carriers were at sea. Over six months Japan took the territories it had attacked, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>.</p><p>The Battle of the Coral Sea in May 1942 turned back an invasion force bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-moresby">Port Moresby</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-midway">Battle of Midway</a> in June is considered a key turning point. Allied offensives began in August 1942 on Guadalcanal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>. The Allied strategy of island hopping followed, with landings in the Gilbert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-islands">Marshall Islands</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Marianas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palau">Palau</a>, Iwo Jima and Okinawa. After the Battle of Leyte Gulf in October 1944 the Japanese fleet turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamikaze">kamikaze</a> attacks. Of the 117,000 Okinawan and Japanese troops defending Okinawa, 94 percent died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> dropped atomic bombs on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> on 6 August 1945 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> on 9 August, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> on 9 August. Japan accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> terms on 15 August and signed the surrender document on 2 September. It lost its possessions in Asia and the Pacific and was occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> until 1952. Total American losses were 161,000 killed and 248,000 wounded. About 2,121,000 Japanese military personnel died between 1937 and 1945, according to a 1964 Japanese report. Japanese forces killed millions of non-combatants, including prisoners of war, and at least 20 million Chinese died during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pacific War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government's ability to restrain military action had effectively collapsed before the <b>Pacific War</b> began.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Pacific War</b> the march was among the pieces used for radio announcements of victories.</blockquote><a href="/songs/battotai">Battōtai</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Pacific War</b> the Japanese army spread the song through the parts of Southeast Asia and the Pacific islands it occupied, and for some forty years after the war many people abroad took it for Japan's representative song.</blockquote><a href="/songs/patriotic-march">Patriotic March</a></li><li><blockquote>What fixed the song in people's minds was its use on the radio in the <b>Pacific War</b>: Imperial General Headquarters announcements that reported the annihilation of units fighting to the last always opened with it, though it was also played for the success of the attack on Pearl…</blockquote><a href="/songs/umi-yukaba">Umi Yukaba</a></li><li><blockquote>American troops came ashore on Okinawa in Operation Iceberg, the largest amphibious assault of the <b>Pacific War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-1">1 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan occupied the Gilberts in 1941; the Battle of Tarawa in 1943 was among the bloodiest of the <b>Pacific War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/gilbert-and-ellice-islands">Gilbert and Ellice Islands</a></li></ul></section>
